We should replace it with code that doesn't error.

This can't really be tested manually.

When performing a semi-automated import, claim notifications are sent to authors whose emails are valid. To minimise confusion, this is only done when importing to Beta, and at some point, this functionality was broken due to a hash key being treated like a method.

*Steps to reproduce*
- Import a work through the API with {{send_emails: true}}
- The import succeeds (the work is imported) but returns the error message:

{code}
An error occurred in the Archive code: undefined method `external_authors' for #<Hash:0x0056058b0a5960>
{code}

The user testy's default pseud has 1116 works visible when logged in. However, [the blurb in testy's pseud list|https://test.archiveofourown.org/users/testy/pseuds] shows 1164 works, and [the blurb in the list of people associated with one of testy's collections|https://test.archiveofourown.org/collections/214186/people] also shows 1164 works. There's a similar difference in work counts when logged out: 524 vs. 565. The difference is presumably the number of anonymous and/or unrevealed works.

Both of these pages call {{Pseud.work_counts_for_pseuds}}, which relies on the scopes {{Pseud.public_work_count_for}} and {{Pseud.posted_work_count_for}}. [The definitions of these two scopes are|https://github.com/otwcode/otwarchive/blob/06c3ccbda022278f243941becdc4c537aed0bea4/app/models/pseud.rb#L160-L174]:

{code:ruby}scope :public_work_count_for, -> (pseud_ids) {
    select('pseuds.id, count(pseuds.id) AS work_count')
      .joins(:works)
      .where(
        pseuds: { id: pseud_ids }, works: { posted: true, hidden_by_admin: false, restricted: false }
      ).group('pseuds.id')
  }


  scope :posted_work_count_for, -> (pseud_ids) {
    select('pseuds.id, count(pseuds.id) AS work_count')
      .joins(:works)
      .where(
        pseuds: { id: pseud_ids }, works: { posted: true, hidden_by_admin: false }
      ).group('pseuds.id')
  }{code}

Note that neither scope checks anonymous/unrevealed status.

The same issue is present on the muted users page and the blocked users page and should be fixed there as well.

h1. Steps to reproduce

# Log in
# Choose an adventure:
## Hi, username\! > My Dashboard > Skins > Create Site Skin
## Hi, username\! > My Dashboard > Skins > My Work Skins > Create Work Skin
# Try to make a skin involving flexbox

h1. What happens

Most of the properties are not allowed. They are removed and you get an error message.

On the bright side, the display property already allows the values flex and inline-flex.

h1. What needs to happen

The following list of properties and values should be allowed.

* {{align-content}} property with values {{flex-start}}, {{flex-end}}, {{center}}, {{space-between}}, {{space-around}}, {{stretch}}
* {{align-items}} property with values {{stretch}}, {{flex-start}}, {{flex-end}}, {{center}}, {{baseline}}
* {{align-self}} property with values {{stretch}}, {{flex-start}}, {{flex-end}}, {{center}}, {{baseline}} (same as {{align-items}} values)
* {{flex}} property with space-separated list of 1 {{flex-grow}} value, 1 {{flex-shrink}} value, and 1 {{flex-basis}} value (only {{flex-grow}} is required)
* {{flex-basis}} property with numerical values with a unit or percentage or {{auto}}, {{fill}}, {{max-content}}, {{min-content}}, {{fit-content}}, {{content}}
* {{flex-direction}} property with values {{row}}, {{row-reverse,}} {{column}}, {{column-reverse}}
* {{flex-flow}} property with space-separated list of 1 {{flex-direction}} value and 1 {{flex-wrap}} value, e.g. {{flex-flow: row wrap}}
* {{flex-grow}} property with unit-less whole number (i.e. no negatives) values, e.g. {{flex-grow: 4}}
* {{flex-shrink}} property with unit-less whole number values, e.g. {{flex-shrink: 1}}
* {{flex-wrap}} property with values {{nowrap}}, {{wrap}}, {{wrap-reverse}}
* {{justify-content}} property with values {{flex-start}}, {{flex-end}}, {{center}}, {{space-between}}, {{space-around}}, {{space-evenly}}
* {{order}} property with unit-less integer values, e.g. {{order: -2}}

Note that there's no need to actually check that the user is putting {{flex-direction: row}} instead of {{flex-direction: 7}} or {{flex-direction: banana}}, though – we don't do that with most values unless we want to specifically forbid something (e.g. a certain type of positioning in work skins). We're not making sure users are writing working CSS; we're just sanitizing things to make sure they're not pulling a little Bobby Tables on us.

h1. Testing

Put the following into a skin and make sure that after saving, the output matches the input and you don't get any error messages. Note that this won't actually do anything useful; it's just to make sure all the values are accepted.

{code:css}div.a {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: row;
  flex-wrap: nowrap;
  justify-content: flex-start;
  align-items: stretch;
  align-content: flex-start;
  order: 3;
  flex-grow: 10;
  flex-shrink: 5;
  flex-basis: auto;
  align-self: stretch;
}

div.b {
  display: inline-flex;
  flex-direction: row-reverse;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  justify-content: flex-end;
  align-items: flex-start;
  align-content: flex-end;
  order: -1;
  flex-basis: fill;
  align-self: flex-start;
}

div.c {
  flex-direction: column;
  flex-wrap: wrap-reverse;
  justify-content: center;
  align-items: flex-end;
  align-content: center;
  flex-basis: max-content;
  align-self: flex-end;
}

div.d {
  flex-direction: column-reverse;
  justify-content: space-between;
  align-items: center;
  align-content: space-between;
  flex-basis: min-content;
  align-self: center;
}

div.e {
  justify-content: space-around;
  align-items: baseline;
  align-content: space-around;
  flex-basis: fit-content;
  align-self: baseline;
}

div.f {
  justify-content: space-evenly;
  align-content: stretch;
  flex-basis: content;
}

div.g {
  flex-basis: 10%;
}

div.h {
  flex-basis: 500px;
}

div.i {
  flex-basis: 5.75rem;
}

div.j {
  flex: 1;
}

div.k {
  flex: 2 1;
}

div.l {
  flex: 3 1 max-content;
}

div.m {
  flex: 5 4 30%;
}

div.n {
  flex: 2 1 15em;
}

div.o {
  flex: 5 15%;
}

div.p {
  flex: 13 fit-content;
}{code}

You’ll also need to check the skins help file – the ? by the CSS field – and make sure the new properties are all listed.

If you put the following {{blockquote}} of some Hebrew text (stolen from [the translation of Naomi's speech at the Hugos|https://archiveofourown.org/admin_posts/13531]) in a work, it will produce a block of text with a border on the left side, which is weird in right to left languages:



{code:html}<blockquote dir="rtl">
  פרס ההוגו הזה יצטרף לנדידתה של התערוכה המגיעה לכל Worldcon, כיוון שהוא שייך לכולנו. אני רוצה לבקש להרים את האורות, ושמי מכם שמרגיש חלק מהקהילה שלנו שיעמוד לרגע ויחלוק אותו איתנו.
</blockquote>{code}



Likewise, a {{blockquote}} included in an FAQ that's written in a RTL language will have the border in the same odd place (these {{blockquote}} elements generally won't have {{dir="rtl"}} on them directly – they get the direction from a parent {{div}}).

Ergo, we need to add some CSS to basically flip the styling we use in LTR languages:



{code:css}.userstuff[dir="rtl"] blockquote:not([dir="ltr"]),
.userstuff blockquote[dir="rtl"] {
  margin: auto 1.5em auto auto;
  border-left: none;
  border-right: 2px solid #999;
}{code}



The first selector should take care of {{blockquote}}s in the FAQ, while the second should take care of {{blockquote}}s in works (and in news posts until [https://otwarchive.atlassian.net/browse/AO3-5735#icft=AO3-5735|https://otwarchive.atlassian.net/browse/AO3-5735#icft=AO3-5735|smart-link] is done).



*Testing notes*

Also try including a {{blockquote}} with the direction set to ltr in a Hebrew or Arabic FAQ and make sure the border is still on the left.



{code:html}<blockquote dir="ltr">
  This is in English. The border should be on the left.
</blockquote>{code}

