Description:
After [https://otwarchive.atlassian.net/browse/AO3-4008#icft=AO3-4008|https://otwarchive.atlassian.net/browse/AO3-4008#icft=AO3-4008|smart-link] this happened, where the first line of the list of kudos names is not cleared of the left of the feedback buttons:  

!image (2).png|width=200,height=183!

This happened as a result of rearranging the contents of {{div#feedback}}. It used to go:

* {{div#kudos_message}}
* comment section {{h3.landmark.heading}}, which has the {{clear: both}} style
* comment flash {{div}}
* {{div#kudos}}

And now it goes:

* {{div#kudos_message}}
* kudos flash {{div}}
* {{div#kudos}}
* comment section {{h3.landmark.heading}}

While we could solve this by applying {{clear: both}} to {{div#kudos}}, instead adding a new landmark ({{<h3 class="landmark heading">Kudos</h3>}}) would allow users who navigate by heading to jump to the kudos section specifically.

We need to manually update the actionpack gem to 5.2.6 due to [CVE-2021-22904|https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-7wjx-3g7j-8584]. 

{quote}*Dependabot cannot update actionpack to a non-vulnerable version*


The latest possible version that can be installed is 5.2.5 because of the following conflicting dependencies:

{{rails (5.2.5) requires actionpack (= 5.2.5) via actioncable (5.2.5)}}
{{rails (5.2.5) requires actionpack (= 5.2.5) via actionmailer (5.2.5)}}
{{rails (5.2.5) requires actionpack (= 5.2.5) via activestorage (5.2.5)}}
{{rails (5.2.5) requires actionpack (= 5.2.5)}}
{{devise (4.7.2) requires actionpack (= 5.2.5) via railties (5.2.5)}}
{{factory_bot_rails (5.0.2) requires actionpack (= 5.2.5) via railties (5.2.5)}}
{{lograge (0.11.2) requires actionpack (= 5.2.5) via railties (5.2.5)}}
{{rails (5.2.5) requires actionpack (= 5.2.5) via railties (5.2.5)}}
{{rspec-rails (3.8.3) requires actionpack (= 5.2.5) via railties (5.2.5)}}

The earliest fixed version is {{5.2.6.}}{quote}

*Testing*

The pull request also updated the i18n and nokigiri gems, so let’s check

* kudos emails work okay (these are i18n’ed)
* the delete account confirmation page looks okay (this is i18n’ed; no need to actually delete)
* posting and importing works with formatting works

Since we upgraded the base image to ruby:2.7.3 we've unintentionally upgraded bundler as well.

This line of the docker file:

{noformat}RUN gem install bundler -v 1.17.3 && bundle install{noformat}

No longer sets the correct version of bundler since the 2.1.4 install that comes with the base image takes precedence.

Probably need to uninstall bundler entirely then reinstall 1.17.3 in the Dockerfile so we match versions with production.

*Reproduce:*

{{docker run -it ruby:2.7.3 bash}}
{{gem install bundler -v 1.17.3}}
{{bundle version}}

Note the version returned is 2.1.4

*Testing Instructions:*

{{docker compose -d --build}}
{{docker compose exec web bundle version}}
{{docker compose exec web bundler version}}

Should always return 1.17.3 as the version

There are a number of unused methods (and one unused scope) in the {{TagSet}} class:
- {{scope :matching}}
- {{with_type_from_redis}}
- {{topmost_tag_type}}
- {{exact_match?}}
- {{no_match?}}
- {{partial_match?}}
- {{is_subset_of?}}
- {{is_superset_of?}}
- {{matching_tags}}

They aren't called from anywhere, so they should be removed.

There are several unused constants, scopes, and methods defined in the {{Prompt}} class:

* the constant {{OPTIONS_TO_SHOW}}
* the constant {{MAX_OPTIONS_FOR_CHECKBOXES}}
* the scope {{unused}}
* the method {{blank?}}
* the class method {{reset_positions_in_collection!}}
* the method {{tag_list}}
* the method {{tag_unlinked_list}}

(Technically, {{tag_list}} is used, but only in {{tag_unlinked_list}}.)

They should all be removed.

There are three unused actions in the {{AutocompleteController}}:
- {{challenge_participants}}
- {{nominated_parents}}
- {{tag_in_fandom}}

They should be removed.

*Testing Instructions:* No manual testing required.

h1. Steps to reproduce

# Log in as a wrangler (e.g., testy) or an admin
# Go to [https://test.archiveofourown.org/tag_wranglers/t3sty|https://test.archiveofourown.org/tag_wranglers/t3sty] (you can use the name of any nonexistent user here)

h1. What happens

A 500 error.

h1. What should happen

A 404.

h1. Notes

You have to be logged in as a wrangler or an admin; if you're not, you'll just get an access denied message (the specific one depends on whether you're logged in or out). 

The error happens because [we basically don't account for the possibility the user might not exist|https://github.com/otwcode/otwarchive/blob/3d246f54ce2009468c47c34eba54d43bd27a8711/app/controllers/tag_wranglers_controller.rb#L38-L51]:

{code:ruby}  def show
    @wrangler = User.find_by(login: params[:id])
    @page_subtitle = @wrangler.login
    @fandoms = @wrangler.fandoms.by_name
    @counts = {}
    [Fandom, Character, Relationship, Freeform].each do |klass|
      @counts[klass.to_s.downcase.pluralize.to_sym] = Rails.cache.fetch("/wrangler/counts/sidebar/#{klass}", race_condition_ttl: 10, expires_in: 1.hour) do
        klass.unwrangled.in_use.count
      end
    end
    @counts[:UnsortedTag] = Rails.cache.fetch("/wrangler/counts/sidebar/UnsortedTag", race_condition_ttl: 10, expires_in: 1.hour) do
      UnsortedTag.count
    end
  end{code}

*Steps to reproduce*

# Log in as an admin 
# Invitations > Invite New Users
# Use the "Give invite codes to current users" section to mass grant invitations, which will trigger an email

*What happens*

The body of the HTML email is like:

{quote}<p>We just wanted to let you know that you have 1 new invitation, which can be used to create a new account at the Archive. You can invite a friend at <a style="color:#990000" href="http://www.example.com/users/USERNAME/invitations">your invitations page</a>.</p>{quote}

{{<p><%= t(".body", count: @total, invitation_page: style_link(t(".invitation_page_link_text"), user_invitations_url(@user))).html_safe %></p>}}

The body of the text email is like:

{quote}We just wanted to let you know that you have 1 new invitation, which can be used to create a new account at the Archive. You can invite a friend at http://www.example.com/users/USERNAME/invitations.{quote}

{{<%= t(".body", count: @total, invitation_page: user_invitations_url(@user)) %>}}

*What should happen*

The text email should be like: 

{quote}We just wanted to let you know that you have 1 new invitation, which can be used to create a new account at the Archive. You can invite a friend at your invitations page (http://www.example.com/users/USERNAME/invitations).{quote}

Both emails should use our [i18n standards|https://github.com/otwcode/otwarchive/wiki/Internationalization-(i18n)-Standards] for naming keys with URLs or links.

*Notes*

This isn't just pedantry; the current version causes issues in languages that don't use spaces. From Rhine:

{quote}As far as I can tell, the Increased Invites mail has %\{invitation_page} as a placeholder in the text and turns it into a hyperlink with something like "your Invitations page" in the HTML version and just the URL in the plain text version.

Since you wouldn't insert a space between the placeholder and the rest of the sentence in Chinese, there isn't one in the plain text version either - meaning that the program you use to display the plain text mail may include the text that follows as part of the URL as well, leading to a faulty link when directly going through that rather than copying the URL. {quote}

